The user said "в этот же заход" — include the "где ещё?" (size/N via strtoll + INT32_MAX check) in the same block. I'm in plan mode, read-only. Let me finalize the plan for this block.

Let me review the current parse_args.c to be precise about what needs changing. I have it from earlier:

```c
static struct ParseResult parse_args_old(int argc, char** argv) {
    ...
    char* end;
    errno = 0;
    long n = strtol(argv[1], &end, 10);
    if (errno == ERANGE || end == argv[1] || *end != '\0') {
        result.error = PE_BADNUMBER;
        return result;
    }
    result.size = (int32_t)n;
    ...
}

static struct ParseResult parse_args_new(int argc, char** argv) {
    ...
    char* end;
    errno = 0;
    long n = strtol(argv[2], &end, 10);
    if (errno == ERANGE || end == argv[2] || *end != '\0') {
        result.error = PE_BADNUMBER;
        return result;
    }
    result.size = (int32_t)n;
    ...
    // seed
    long s = strtol(argv[4], &end, 10);
    if (errno == ERANGE || end == argv[4] || *end != '\0' || s < 0 || s > UINT32_MAX) {
        result.error = PE_BADSEED;
        return result;
    }
    result.seed = (uint32_t)s;
    ...
}
```

So for the "где ещё?" fix:
- Both `parse_args_old` and `parse_args_new` parse N via `long n = strtol(...)` then `(int32_t)n`.

The fix to make C match C++ behavior (from_chars<int32_t> rejects out-of-range cleanly) cross-platform:
- Change to `long long n = strtoll(argv[1], &end, 10);` and add a range check `n > INT32_MAX` (and `n < INT32_MIN`? For the positional/size mode, negative N is accepted by parse_args (test_negative_number expects -5 accepted at parse level, then main rejects `args.size < 1`). So we should NOT reject negatives at parse level — the existing tests `test_negative_number` check that `-5` is accepted by parse_args. So the range check should only reject values that can't be represented in int32_t: `n > INT32_MAX || n < INT32_MIN`. Hmm, but -5 fits in int32, so accepted. `--size 3000000000` → > INT32_MAX → reject. `--size -2147483649` → < INT32_MIN → reject (currently on Windows strtol would ERANGE for that, on Linux would truncate). 

Wait — but the C++ side: `int32_t size{}; std::from_chars(args[2].data(), ..., size);` — from_chars<int32_t> accepts values in [INT32_MIN, INT32_MAX]. Negative values are accepted (e.g., -5). So C++ parse accepts -5 too. So C should match: accept any int32-representable value, reject outside. So check `n < INT32_MIN || n > INT32_MAX` → PE_BADNUMBER.

Actually wait — let me check: does C++ from_chars<int32_t>("-5") succeed? Yes, -5 is in int32 range. And test in ppm_test.cpp `test_parse_args_negative_number` checks `-5 -> size == -5` accepted. So C++ accepts negative N at parse level. So C strtoll + `n < INT32_MIN || n > INT32_MAX` matches.

So the full C parse_args.c changes:
1. `parse_args_old`: `long n = strtol(...)` → `long long n = strtoll(...)`, check `n < INT32_MIN || n > INT32_MAX` → PE_BADNUMBER, then `result.size = (int32_t)n;`.
2. `parse_args_new`: same for N.
3. `parse_args_new` seed: `long s = strtol(...)` → `long long s = strtoll(...)` (keep the `s < 0 || s > UINT32_MAX` check).

Note: `INT32_MIN`/`INT32_MAX` need `<stdint.h>` — parse_args.c includes parse_args.h which includes `<stdint.h>`. Good. Or `INT32_MIN`/`INT32_MAX` are in `<stdint.h>` as macros. Yes.

Also — need to double check: with strtoll and the check, what about ERANGE? strtoll sets ERANGE only for values beyond long long range (~9.2e18), which the INT32 range check already covers. But keeping `errno == ERANGE` is harmless (defense in depth) — actually for values between INT32_MAX+1 and LLONG_MAX, strtoll won't set ERANGE, so the `n > INT32_MAX` check catches them. For values beyond LLONG_MAX, ERANGE catches. Both paths → PE_BADNUMBER. Good.

Now — the C++ side already handles all this correctly (from_chars<int32_t>, from_chars<uint32_t>). So only C changes.

Tests to add:
- ppm_test.c (C): boundary tests for seed (4294967295 accepted, 4294967296 rejected) AND size boundary (e.g., `--size 3000000000` → PE_BADNUMBER; and maybe `--size 2147483647` accepted? Actually a huge accepted value would try to allocate... but parse_args just stores it; main would reject or attempt generation. At parse level, `--size 2147483647` → accepted (PE_OK). But we don't need to test the max accepted size necessarily. Let me add: `--size 3000000000` → PE_BADNUMBER (out of int32 range). That locks the cross-platform fix. 
- ppm_test.cpp (C++): mirror — `--size 3000000000` → kBadNumber, `--seed 4294967295` → ok, `--seed 4294967296` → kBadSeed.

Wait — does the C++ ppm_test.cpp already have a test for `--size 3000000000`? Let me recall — test_new_bad_size tests "abc". No numeric overflow test. So add boundary tests to both.

Let me also double check: `--seed 4294967295` in C: strtoll parses 4294967295 (fits long long), s < 0 false, s > UINT32_MAX false (equal) → OK → seed = (uint32_t)4294967295. Good.

Hmm — one more: the C++ from_chars<uint32_t> on "4294967295" → success. On "4294967296" → result_out_of_range → kBadSeed. Good.

Now, also should I consider: the positional mode `gen_image 2147483647 gradient` — N = 2147483647 accepted at parse, then main: `args.pattern != RANDOM && args.size > 512` → 2147483647 > 512 → EC_USAGE "N должно быть в [1; 512]". So it's rejected in main. Fine. The --size mode: `gen_image --size 2147483647` → pattern RANDOM → main's `args.size > 512` check skipped (pattern==RANDOM) → generate_ppm with size 2147483647 → tries to write 4.6e18 pixels → hangs/crashes. But that's pre-existing behavior (huge --size), not something to fix here — the mass-generation mode is meant for large sizes anyway. Not in scope. But note: with my strtoll change, `--size 2147483648` (just above int32 max) → now PE_BADNUMBER in C (was ERANGE on Windows before too, so behavior on Windows unchanged; on Linux it now rejects instead of truncating — the actual improvement).

Wait, actually on Windows before my change: `--size 2147483648` → strtol → ERANGE → PE_BADNUMBER. After: strtoll parses 2147483648, `n > INT32_MAX` → PE_BADNUMBER. Same result on Windows. On Linux before: strtol (64-bit long) parses 2147483648 → (int32_t)2147483648 = -2147483648 → stored, later main rejects (size<1? no, -2147483648 < 1 → "размер должен быть положительным"). So on Linux before: confusing "размер должен быть положительным; получено: -2147483648" instead of a clean PE_BADNUMBER. After: clean PE_BADNUMBER. So the fix improves Linux behavior and keeps Windows identical. Good.

So the plan is finalized. Let me present it. The user said "в этот же заход" — so include the size fix. I should present the complete final plan and ask them to exit plan mode.

Let me also double check the C++ side doesn't need changes — from_chars<int32_t> for size is already correct. Right. Only C changes + tests on both sides.

Let me also reconsider: does ppm_test.c need to verify `strtoll` handles the boundary `4294967295` correctly on MSVC? Yes, strtoll is standard. Fine.

Let me present the final plan concisely.

Files to change:
1. `01-image-gen/c/parse_args.c`:
   - parse_args_old: `long n = strtol(...)` → `long long n = strtoll(...)`, add `n < INT32_MIN || n > INT32_MAX` to the error condition.
   - parse_args_new: same for N.
   - parse_args_new seed: `long s = strtol(...)` → `long long s = strtoll(...)`.
2. Tests:
   - `01-image-gen/c/ppm_test.c`: add `test_new_size_overflow` (`--size 3000000000` → PE_BADNUMBER), `test_new_seed_max` (`--seed 4294967295` → PE_OK + seed==4294967295), `test_new_seed_overflow` (`--seed 4294967296` → PE_BADSEED).
   - `01-image-gen/cpp/ppm_test.cpp`: mirror.
3. Verification on user.

Also note: no README changes needed (the seed diagnostic already says [0; 4294967295], which becomes true after the fix). No diagnostic text changes.

